<TopicPage slug="subjects/philosophy/intermediate/philosophy-of-science">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Philosophy of science examines <strong>science's own foundations: what makes it
    knowledge, how theories are confirmed, whether scientific claims describe reality or just
    predict it</strong>. Far from abstract, it settled practical questions — what counts as
    evidence, when to abandon a theory, why replication matters — that every research field now
    uses.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="demarcation, induction, paradigms, realism" widget="equationStep" config={sciCfg} />

  <h2>Case studies that keep it honest</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The Duhem–Quine problem:</strong> experiments test bundles of theory + equipment + assumptions — a failed prediction never tells you WHICH part failed. This is why replication and multiple methods matter.</li>
    <li><strong>Underdetermination:</strong> multiple incompatible theories can fit all data (wave vs matrix mechanics, historically). Values like simplicity and unification do the choosing — science's hidden value judgements.</li>
    <li><strong>Feyerabend's provocation:</strong> "anything goes" — historical science was messier than the method stories admit. The useful reading: methods evolve with the science, not before it.</li>
    <li><strong>Applied constantly today:</strong> p-value debates, climate-model trust, AI benchmark validity — each is philosophy-of-science in the news. The research-methods lesson (psychology ladder) is this subject operationalised.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Popper, Logic of Scientific Discovery (1959); Kuhn (1962); Godfrey-Smith, Theory and Reality; Okasha, Philosophy of Science: A Very Short Introduction.</p>
</TopicPage>
